{
  "_comment": [
    "Bridges the detector's class vocabulary to the language recipes are written in.",
    "Keys are detector class names as they appear in the YOLO checkpoint (model.names).",
    "  aliases   - extra surface forms to match inside recipe ingredient strings.",
    "              The dataset merges FV40 with a Nepali ingredient set, so several",
    "              classes are romanised Nepali that no English recipe will ever spell",
    "              that way; the alias is what actually earns the match.",
    "  canonical - merge this class with a synonym class under one key. 'palak' and",
    "              'palungo' are both spinach; without this they are separate keys and",
    "              only one of them can own the phrase 'spinach'.",
    "  display   - what the UI shows instead of the raw class name.",
    "  category  - drives the coloured chips in the ingredient panel.",
    "Classes absent from this file still work: they match on their own name and fall",
    "back to keyword-based category inference in taxonomy.py.",
    "NOTE: never alias a phrase that is another class's own name (e.g. do not give",
    "'citrus' an alias of 'lemon' when 'lemon' is its own class) - taxonomy.py now",
    "ignores such aliases rather than letting them orphan the real class."
  ],

  "palungo":         { "canonical": "spinach", "aliases": ["spinach"], "display": "Spinach", "category": "vegetable" },
  "palak":           { "canonical": "spinach", "aliases": ["spinach"], "display": "Spinach", "category": "vegetable" },
